    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n- Model the uncovered squares as a permutation $p$ of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$, $n=2025$, where the uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $p(i)$.\n- Decompose the set of covered cells into a *left* part $L_i=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\}$ and a *right* part $R_i=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}$.\n- Prove that any rectangle lying entirely in $\\bigcup_i L_i$ must be of the form $I\\times[1,k]$ for some $k$, where $I$ is a set of consecutive rows with $p(i)>k$ for all $i\\in I$; similarly any rectangle in $\\bigcup_i R_i$ must be of the form $I\\times[k,n]$.\n- Show that for each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ there is a unique row $i_k$ with $p(i_k)=k+1$. The cell $(i_k,k)$ is the rightmost cell of $L_{i_k}$ and forces a distinct rectangle whose rightmost column is $k$. Hence at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed on the left side.\n- By symmetry, at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed on the right side, giving a lower bound of $2(n-1)$ tiles.\n- Construct a tiling attaining this bound by choosing the diagonal permutation $p(i)=i$ and, for each row $i>1$, placing a $1\\times(i-1)$ tile covering columns $1$ through $i-1$, and for each row $i<n$, placing a $1\\times(n-i)$ tile covering columns $i+1$ through $n$. This uses exactly $2(n-1)$ tiles.\n- Conclude that the minimum number of tiles is $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$.  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation $p:\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ such that the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,p(i))$.  All other $n^2-n$ unit squares must be covered by axis\u2011aligned rectangular tiles, no two of which overlap.\n\n#### 1.  Decomposition of the board\nFor each row $i$ define\n\\[\nL_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad R_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThe set of all covered squares is $\\bigcup_{i=1}^n L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^n R_i$, and the families $\\{L_i\\}$ and $\\{R_i\\}$ are disjoint.\n\n#### 2.  Shape of a rectangle inside the left side\nConsider a rectangle $T$ that lies entirely inside $\\bigcup_i L_i$.  Write $T=I\\times J$, where $I$ is a set of consecutive rows and $J$ a set of consecutive columns.  If $(i,j)\\in T$, then $j\\in J$ and $j<p(i)$.  Hence $\\max J < p(i)$ for every $i\\in I$, i.e.\n\\[\n\\max J \\le \\min_{i\\in I}\\bigl(p(i)-1\\bigr).\n\\]\nConsequently $J$ must be an initial segment $\\{1,2,\\dots,k\\}$ for some $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$, and every row $i\\in I$ satisfies $p(i)>k$.  Conversely, for any $k$ and any set $I$ of consecutive rows with $p(i)>k$ for all $i\\in I$, the rectangle $I\\times\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$ is contained in $\\bigcup_i L_i$.  Thus each rectangle in the left side is uniquely identified by its rightmost column $k$.\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left side\nFor each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ there is a unique row $i_k$ such that $p(i_k)=k+1$ (since $p$ is a permutation).  In that row the cell $(i_k,k)$ is the rightmost cell of $L_{i_k}$.  Any rectangle covering $(i_k,k)$ must have rightmost column $k$: if its rightmost column were $<k$, the cell would not be covered; if it were $>k$, the rectangle would also contain column $k+1$ in row $i_k$, which is the uncovered square.  Hence $(i_k,k)$ forces a rectangle whose rightmost column is exactly $k$.  Different values of $k$ require rectangles with different rightmost columns, so at least $n-1$ rectangles are necessary to cover $\\bigcup_i L_i$.\n\n#### 4.  The right side\nA symmetric argument applied to the sets $R_i$ shows that any rectangle inside $\\bigcup_i R_i$ must be of the form $I\\times\\{k,\\dots,n\\}$ for some $k\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$.  For each $k\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$ there is a unique row $i'_k$ with $p(i'_k)=k-1$.  The cell $(i'_k,k)$ is the leftmost cell of $R_{i'_k}$ and forces a rectangle whose leftmost column is exactly $k$.  Hence at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed for the right side.\n\n#### 5.  Global lower bound\nSince the left\u2011side rectangles and the right\u2011side rectangles are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board must contain at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\nrectangles.\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\nChoose the diagonal permutation $p(i)=i$ for all $i$.  For each row $i>1$ place a tile covering the cells $(i,1),\\dots,(i,i-1)$; this is a $1\\times(i-1)$ rectangle.  For each row $i<n$ place a tile covering the cells $(i,i+1),\\dots,(i,n)$, a $1\\times(n-i)$ rectangle.  These tiles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every cell except the diagonal squares $(i,i)$, and their number is\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\]\nThus the lower bound is tight.\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion for $n=2025$\nSubstituting $n=2025$ gives the minimum number of tiles\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nTherefore Matilda needs at least $4048$ tiles, and $4048$ tiles are sufficient.\n\n### End of Detailed Solution ###\n",
