    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n- **Lower bound (2n\u20112):** For each row $i$ let $p(i)$ be the column of the uncovered unit square (hole).  Define the sets of left\u2011neighbor cells $L=\\{(i,p(i)-1)\\mid p(i)>1\\}$ and right\u2011neighbor cells $R=\\{(i,p(i)+1)\\mid p(i)<n\\}$.  Both $L$ and $R$ have size $n-1$.  A rectangular tile cannot contain two distinct cells of $L$, cannot contain two distinct cells of $R$, and cannot contain a cell of $L$ together with a cell of $R$ (otherwise the tile would also contain a hole).  Hence at least $n-1$ tiles are needed for $L$ and at least $n-1$ tiles for $R$, and the two families of tiles must be disjoint.  Therefore any admissible tiling uses at least $2n-2$ tiles.\n- **Construction achieving $2n-2$ tiles:** Use the *cyclic shift* permutation $p(i)=i+1$ for $1\\le i\\le n-1$ and $p(n)=1$.  For each $i=1,\\dots,n-1$ place a vertical $1\\times (n-i+1)$ tile $V_i$ covering column $i$ and rows $i,\\dots,n$ (for $i=1$ we use rows $1,\\dots,n-1$ to avoid the hole at $(n,1)$).  For each $i=1,\\dots,n-1$ place a horizontal $(n-i-1)\\times1$ tile $H_i$ covering row $i$ and columns $i+2,\\dots,n$.  The $V_i$'s are pairwise disjoint, the $H_i$'s are pairwise disjoint, and a $V_i$ never meets an $H_j$.  Together they cover every non\u2011hole cell exactly once, using $ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles.\n- **Conclusion:** The lower bound $2n-2$ is tight, so the minimum possible number of tiles is $2\\cdot2025-2=\\boxed{4048}$.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$ and consider an $n\\times n$ board whose rows are numbered $1,2,\\dots ,n$ from top to bottom and whose columns are numbered $1,2,\\dots ,n$ from left to right.\n\n#### 1.  Formalisation of the problem\nMatilda chooses a set $H$ of $n$ unit squares, one in each row and one in each column; we call the squares in $H$ *holes*.  Every other unit square must be covered by a rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines, and no two tiles may overlap.\n\nFor a row $i$ let $p(i)$ denote the column of the hole in that row.  The map $p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ is a permutation because each column contains exactly one hole.\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound of $2n-2$\nDefine the two sets\n\\[\nL=\\{\\,(i,\\,p(i)-1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)>1\\,\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{\\,(i,\\,p(i)+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nBoth $L$ and $R$ have size $n-1$: exactly one row has $p(i)=1$ (hence no element of $L$) and exactly one row has $p(i)=n$ (hence no element of $R$).\n\n**Lemma 1.** A single axis\u2011aligned rectangle can contain at most one element of $L$.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle $R$ contains two distinct cells $(i_1,p(i_1)-1)$ and $(i_2,p(i_2)-1)$ with $i_1<i_2$.  Its column interval therefore contains both $p(i_1)-1$ and $p(i_2)-1$, and being contiguous it also contains $p(i_1)=p(i_1)-1+1$.  Consequently $R$ would contain the hole $(i_1,p(i_1))$, contradicting the rules.  \u220e\n\n**Lemma 2.** A single rectangle can contain at most one element of $R$.\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is identical to Lemma\u00a01, using the cells $(i,p(i)+1)$ and the fact that the interval then contains $p(i)$.  \u220e\n\n**Lemma 3.** No rectangle can contain simultaneously an element of $L$ and an element of $R$.\n\n*Proof.*  Assume $R$ contains $(i,p(i)-1)\\in L$ and $(j,p(j)+1)\\in R$, and without loss of generality $i\\le j$.  The column interval of $R$ contains $p(i)-1$ and $p(j)+1$.  Since $p(i)-1<p(i)\\le p(j)<p(j)+1$, the interval also contains $p(i)$ and $p(j)$.  Hence $R$ would contain the holes $(i,p(i))$ and $(j,p(j))$, a contradiction.  \u220e\n\nFrom Lemmas\u00a01\u20133 we deduce that each tile can cover at most one cell of $L$ and at most one cell of $R$, and a tile covering a cell of $L$ cannot cover any cell of $R$.  Therefore we need at least $|L|=n-1$ tiles to cover all cells of $L$ and at least $|R|=n-1$ tiles to cover all cells of $R$, and the two families of tiles must be disjoint.  Hence any admissible tiling uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n#### 3.  A construction with $2n-2$ tiles\nTake the *cyclic shift* permutation\n\\[\np(i)=\\begin{cases}i+1,&1\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2pt]1,&i=n.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus the holes are at\n\\[(1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n),(n,1).\\]\nFor each $i=1,\\dots ,n-1$ we place two tiles:\n\n*Vertical tile* $V_i$: the column $i$ together with the rows $i,i+1,\\dots ,n$.\n\\[V_i=\\{\\,(r,i)\\mid i\\le r\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nFor $i=1$ we omit the last row $r=n$ (otherwise $V_1$ would contain the hole $(n,1)$); i.e. $V_1$ consists of the cells $(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n-1,1)$.  In every case $V_i$ contains the left\u2011neighbor cell $(i,i)$ (which belongs to $L$) and no hole, because the only hole in column $i$ lies in row $i-1$ (or in row $n$ when $i=1$), which is above the top row of $V_i$.\n\n*Horizontal tile* $H_i$: the row $i$ together with the columns $i+2,i+3,\\dots ,n$.\n\\[H_i=\\{\\,(i,c)\\mid i+2\\le c\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThese tiles contain exactly the cells to the right of the hole in row $i$; they never contain a hole because the hole of row $i$ is at column $i+1$, which is omitted.\n\nThe families $\\{V_i\\}$ and $\\{H_i\\}$ are pairwise disjoint:\n- Two different vertical tiles $V_i$ and $V_j$ lie in different columns, so they cannot intersect.\n- Two different horizontal tiles $H_i$ and $H_j$ lie in different rows, so they cannot intersect.\n- A vertical tile $V_i$ occupies column $i$, whereas a horizontal tile $H_j$ occupies columns $j+2,\\dots ,n$; since $i\\le j$ implies $i\\le j+1$, column $i$ is never among $j+2,\\dots ,n$.  Hence $V_i\\cap H_j=\\emptyset$ for all $i,j$.\n\nNow we verify that every non\u2011hole cell is covered.\n- A cell $(r,c)$ with $c\\le r$ lies in column $c$ and row $r\\ge c$, so it belongs to the vertical tile $V_c$.\n- A cell $(r,c)$ with $c\\ge r+2$ lies in row $r$ and column $c\\ge r+2$, so it belongs to the horizontal tile $H_r$.\n- The remaining cells are exactly the holes $(i,i+1)$ (for $1\\le i\\le n-1$) and $(n,1)$, which are intentionally left uncovered.\nThus the $2(n-1)$ tiles $V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}$ partition the complement of the holes.\n\nThe number of tiles used is\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=\\boxed{4048}.\\]\n\n#### 4.  Minimality\nSection\u00a02 proved that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while Section\u00a03 exhibited a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.\n\n### Final Answer ###\nThe minimum number of tiles required is \\boxed{4048}.\n"
}
