t squares indexed by $(i,j)$ with $1\\\\le i,j\\\\le n$.  Matilda chooses a permutation $\\\\pi$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ and leaves uncovered exactly the squares $(i,\\\\pi(i))$; every row and every column therefore contains precisely one uncovered square.\\n\\n#### 1.  Graph formulation\\nConsider the bipartite graph $G$ with left vertex set $R=\\\\{r_1,\\\\dots,r_n\\\\}$ (rows) and right vertex set $C=\\\\{c_1,\\\\dots,c_n\\\\}$ (columns).  An edge $r_i c_j$ is present iff the square $(i,j)$ is covered.  Hence\\n\\\\[\\nE(G)=\\\\{\\\\,r_i c_j\\\\mid j\\\\neq \\\\pi(i)\\\\,\\\\}=K_{n,n}\\\\setminus M,\\n\\\\]\\nwhere $M=\\\\{\\\\,r_i c_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ is a perfect matching (the uncovered squares).\\n\\nA rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines corresponds exactly to a complete bipartite subgraph (a *biclique*) $K_{R,C}$ with $R\\\\subseteq R$, $C\\\\subseteq C$; the rectangle consists of all squares $(i,j)$ with $i\\\\in R$, $j\\\\in C$.  Because a tile may not contain an uncovered square, the biclique $K_{R,C}$ must satisfy $c_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\notin C$ for every $r_i\\\\in R$.\\n\\nThus the problem is to partition $E(G)$ into the smallest possible number of bicliques satisfying the above restriction.\\n\\n#### 2.  A family of edge‑cuts\\nFor $k\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ define\\n\\\\[\\nL_k = \\\\{\\\\,r_i c_j\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)\\\\le k < j\\\\,\\\\},\\\\qquad\\nR_k = \\\\{\\\\,r_i c_j\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)>k\\\\ge j\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n$L_k$ consists of all edges that cross the vertical line between columns $k$ and $k+1$ from left to right; $R_k$ consists of the edges crossing the same line from right to left.  Because exactly $k$ rows have their matched column $\\\\le k$, each $L_k$ is the complete bipartite graph $K_{k,\\\\,n-k}$, and similarly $R_k$ is $K_{n-k,\\\\,k}$.\\n\\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of bicliques\\nLet $\\\\mathcal{B}$ be any family of bicliques that partitions $E(G)$.  Take a biclique $B=K_{R,C}\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}$ and suppose it contains an edge $r_i c_j\\\\in L_k$ for some $k$.  By definition $\\\\pi(i)\\\\le k<j$.  Since $c_j\\\\in C$, the condition $c_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\notin C$ forces $\\\\pi(i)\\\\notin C$.  Because $\\\\pi(i)\\\\le k<j$ and $j\\\\in C$, we have $\\\\pi(i)\\\\notin C$ automatically; however, if $B$ also contained a row $r_{i'}$ with $\\\\pi(i')>k$, then $\\\\pi(i')$ would lie in the column interval $C$ (which contains $j>k$) and the edge $r_{i'}c_{\\\\pi(i')}$ would be a forbidden edge of $M$, contradicting the definition of a tile.  Consequently **every row of $B$ satisfies $\\\\pi(i)\\\\le k$** and **every column of $B$ satisfies $j>k$**.  Hence $B\\\\subseteq L_k$.\\n\\nThus any biclique that covers an edge of $L_k$ is completely contained in $L_k$.  Since the sets $L_1,\\\\dots,L_{n-1}$ are pairwise edge‑disjoint and together contain all edges that go from a row whose hole lies left of the cut to a column on the right, each $L_k$ must be covered by at least one biclique of $\\\\mathcal{B}$.  The same reasoning applied to the right‑crossing sets $R_k$ shows that each $R_k$ also requires a distinct biclique.\\n\\nThere are $n-1$ left‑crossing sets and $n-1$ right‑crossing sets, all disjoint.  Hence\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\mathcal{B}|\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nSo any tiling needs at least $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound\\nChoose the identity permutation $\\\\pi(i)=i$ (any permutation works, but the identity makes the construction transparent).  For each $k=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ place the two rectangles\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{(a)}\\\\;[1,k]\\\\times[k+1,n],\\\\qquad\\n\\\\text{(b)}\\\\;(k+1,n]\\\\times[1,k].\\n\\\\]\\nRectangle (a) consists of rows $1$ through $k$ and columns $k+1$ through $n$; rectangle (b) consists of rows $k+1$ through $n$ and columns $1$ through $k$.  Because the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells $(i,i)$, neither rectangle contains a diagonal cell, so all rectangles are admissible tiles.\\n