athcal{B}$ and suppose it contains an edge $r_i c_j\\\\in L_k$ for some $k$.  By definition $\\\\pi(i)\\\\le k<j$.  Since $c_j\\\\in C$, the condition $c_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\notin C$ forces $\\\\pi(i)\\\\notin C$.  Because $\\\\pi(i)\\\\le k<j$ and $j\\\\in C$, we have $\\\\pi(i)\\\\notin C$ automatically; however, if $B$ also contained a row $r_{i'}$ with $\\\\pi(i')>k$, then $\\\\pi(i')$ would lie in the column interval $C$ (which contains $j>k$) and the edge $r_{i'}c_{\\\\pi(i')}$ would be a forbidden edge of $M$, contradicting the definition of a tile.  Consequently **every row of $B$ satisfies $\\\\pi(i)\\\\le k$** and **every column of $B$ satisfies $j>k$**.  Hence $B\\\\subseteq L_k$.\\n\\nThus any biclique that covers an edge of $L_k$ is completely contained in $L_k$.  Since the sets $L_1,\\\\dots,L_{n-1}$ are pairwise edge‑disjoint and together contain all edges that go from a row whose hole lies left of the cut to a column on the right, each $L_k$ must be covered by at least one biclique of $\\\\mathcal{B}$.  The same reasoning applied to the right‑crossing sets $R_k$ shows that each $R_k$ also requires a distinct biclique.\\n\\nThere are $n-1$ left‑crossing sets and $n-1$ right‑crossing sets, all disjoint.  Hence\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\mathcal{B}|\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nSo any tiling needs at least $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound\\nChoose the identity permutation $\\\\pi(i)=i$ (any permutation works, but the identity makes the construction transparent).  For each $k=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ place the two rectangles\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{(a)}\\\\;[1,k]\\\\times[k+1,n],\\\\qquad\\n\\\\text{(b)}\\\\;(k+1,n]\\\\times[1,k].\\n\\\\]\\nRectangle (a) consists of rows $1$ through $k$ and columns $k+1$ through $n$; rectangle (b) consists of rows $k+1$ through $n$ and columns $1$ through $k$.  Because the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells $(i,i)$, neither rectangle contains a diagonal cell, so all rectangles are admissible tiles.\\n\\nThe $2(n-1)$ rectangles are pairwise disjoint: a rectangle of type (a) occupies only columns larger than its row index, while a rectangle of type (b) occupies only columns smaller than its row index.  Every covered square $(i,j)$ with $j\\\\neq i$ belongs either to a rectangle of type (a) (if $i<j$) or to a rectangle of type (b) (if $i>j$).  Hence the $2(n-1)$ tiles cover exactly all squares that are allowed to be covered, and no two tiles overlap.\\n\\nThus a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\\n\\n#### 5.  Minimality\\nThe lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles proved in Section 3 matches the explicit construction of Section 4.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly $2n-2$.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore Matilda must place at least $4048$ tiles, and this number is attainable.\\n\\n**Answer:** \\\\boxed{4048}\\n\"}"
